1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,097
Every business answers one question at the end of the year.

2
00:00:04,097 --> 00:00:05,555
Did we make a profit?

3
00:00:05,555 --> 00:00:08,471
A nonprofit has to answer a different one.

4
00:00:08,471 --> 00:00:11,457
Did we use each dollar the way we promised?

5
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:13,679
Those are not the same question.

6
00:00:13,679 --> 00:00:17,568
And that gap is the whole reason fund accounting exists.

7
00:00:17,568 --> 00:00:23,331
If you run a nonprofit, you've probably seen a report full of words like restricted

8
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:25,345
unrestricted, and net assets.

9
00:00:25,345 --> 00:00:27,220
Most people just nod along.

10
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:33,191
Today I'll explain what those actually mean, in plain English, in about seven minutes.

11
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:35,066
No jargon for its own sake.

12
00:00:35,066 --> 00:00:38,260
And no pitch to go hire a big accounting firm.

13
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,760
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

14
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:43,121
I build grant compliance software.

15
00:00:43,121 --> 00:00:49,578
To make a computer follow these rules, I had to learn exactly how the money is meant to move.

16
00:00:49,578 --> 00:00:52,773
I'm not an auditor, and I won't talk like one.

17
00:00:52,773 --> 00:00:56,450
You're getting the version a busy director actually needs.

18
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:57,390
So what is it?

19
00:00:57,390 --> 00:01:02,022
Fund accounting is a way of tracking money by purpose, not by profit.

20
00:01:02,022 --> 00:01:06,722
Instead of pooling everything into one pot and asking what's left over

21
00:01:06,722 --> 00:01:09,475
you split your money into separate funds.

22
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:12,227
Each fund is its own little set of books.

23
00:01:12,227 --> 00:01:15,786
Each one has its own rules about what it can pay for.

24
00:01:15,786 --> 00:01:19,747
Picture one bank account versus a row of labeled envelopes.

25
00:01:19,747 --> 00:01:24,044
A regular business runs the one account and watches the balance.

26
00:01:24,044 --> 00:01:26,125
A nonprofit runs the envelopes.

27
00:01:26,125 --> 00:01:28,609
This one is the after-school program.

28
00:01:28,609 --> 00:01:30,288
This one is the building.

29
00:01:30,288 --> 00:01:33,242
This one is money you can spend on anything.

30
00:01:33,242 --> 00:01:35,659
The cash might sit in the same bank.

31
00:01:35,659 --> 00:01:39,821
But on paper, every dollar knows which envelope it belongs to.

32
00:01:39,821 --> 00:01:42,641
The point isn't to make accounting harder.

33
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:48,885
The point is accountability, which just means being able to show what you did with the money.

34
00:01:48,885 --> 00:01:51,906
Fund accounting is built to answer one thing.

35
00:01:51,906 --> 00:01:54,980
Can you prove every dollar did the job you gave it?

36
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:59,705
Here's why a normal profit-and-loss report doesn't work for a nonprofit.

37
00:01:59,705 --> 00:02:02,133
Most of the money comes with strings.

38
00:02:02,133 --> 00:02:07,449
A funder gives you fifty thousand dollars, but only for the after-school program.

39
00:02:07,449 --> 00:02:11,124
A donor leaves money for the building, and nothing else.

40
00:02:11,124 --> 00:02:13,158
That's called restricted money.

41
00:02:13,158 --> 00:02:16,505
You can spend it, but only on the thing they named.

42
00:02:16,505 --> 00:02:20,508
A regular income statement just shows money in and money out.

43
00:02:20,508 --> 00:02:23,527
It can't show whether you kept those promises.

44
00:02:23,527 --> 00:02:24,839
Fund accounting can.

45
00:02:24,839 --> 00:02:28,121
It keeps the restricted money in its own envelope.

46
00:02:28,121 --> 00:02:33,568
So at any moment, you can show that those dollars went where they were supposed to.

47
00:02:33,568 --> 00:02:35,733
And that question does get asked.

48
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:39,999
When a funder or an auditor reviews you, they look for one thing.

49
00:02:39,999 --> 00:02:43,674
Did the restricted money pay for its restricted purpose?

50
00:02:43,674 --> 00:02:46,824
If you can't show it, that's where trust breaks.

51
00:02:46,824 --> 00:02:49,230
Fund accounting is what makes it provable.

52
00:02:49,230 --> 00:02:51,086
Okay, the building blocks.

53
00:02:51,086 --> 00:02:54,583
Money in a nonprofit gets sorted into two groups.

54
00:02:54,583 --> 00:03:01,791
The official names are net assets without donor restrictions, and net assets with donor restrictions.

55
00:03:01,791 --> 00:03:03,932
Don't let the words scare you.

56
00:03:03,932 --> 00:03:06,787
It's just free money and promised money.

57
00:03:06,787 --> 00:03:10,284
Without donor restrictions is the flexible money.

58
00:03:10,284 --> 00:03:13,638
You can spend it on whatever the mission needs.

59
00:03:13,638 --> 00:03:15,565
Salaries, rent, the lights.

60
00:03:15,565 --> 00:03:21,560
With donor restrictions is money a donor or grantor tied to a purpose, or to a time.

61
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,986
The after-school grant lives here.

62
00:03:23,986 --> 00:03:28,483
So does a gift you're asked to hold forever, like an endowment.

63
00:03:28,483 --> 00:03:33,050
If you've read older articles, you might have seen three groups.

64
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:37,689
Unrestricted, temporarily restricted, and permanently restricted.

65
00:03:37,689 --> 00:03:39,259
That was the old rule.

66
00:03:39,259 --> 00:03:46,681
The standards changed it to just two groups, for financial years that start after late twenty seventeen.

67
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:50,250
So if a guide still lists three, it's out of date.

68
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:51,249
There are two.

69
00:03:51,249 --> 00:03:52,248
One more trap.

70
00:03:52,248 --> 00:03:55,674
Say your board sets aside money for a rainy day.

71
00:03:55,674 --> 00:03:57,743
That is not restricted money.

72
00:03:57,743 --> 00:04:00,598
The board can change its own mind later.

73
00:04:00,598 --> 00:04:04,024
Only an outside donor can truly restrict a gift.

74
00:04:04,024 --> 00:04:06,600
Board savings stay in the flexible group.

75
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:09,633
Let me make this real with a tiny example.

76
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:13,244
Say a nonprofit gets two pots of money this month.

77
00:04:13,244 --> 00:04:18,299
A fifty-thousand-dollar grant, restricted to the after-school program.

78
00:04:18,299 --> 00:04:22,488
And thirty thousand in general donations, with no strings.

79
00:04:22,488 --> 00:04:25,232
In fund accounting, those don't merge.

80
00:04:25,232 --> 00:04:28,915
The fifty thousand goes into the after-school fund.

81
00:04:28,915 --> 00:04:32,309
The thirty thousand goes into the general fund.

82
00:04:32,309 --> 00:04:37,292
Now say the program is running fine, but the office is short on rent.

83
00:04:37,292 --> 00:04:41,264
Can you borrow from the after-school grant to cover it?

84
00:04:41,264 --> 00:04:41,481
No.

85
00:04:41,481 --> 00:04:43,142
That money is promised.

86
00:04:43,142 --> 00:04:46,608
Spending it on rent would break the restriction.

87
00:04:46,608 --> 00:04:47,908
And it would show.

88
00:04:47,908 --> 00:04:50,580
That's the whole idea in one picture.

89
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:55,274
Two funds, two sets of rules, and a line you can't quietly cross.

90
00:04:55,274 --> 00:04:59,896
Later, the after-school program spends its grant on the program.

91
00:04:59,896 --> 00:05:04,085
The promise is kept, and that money is now free and clear.

92
00:05:04,085 --> 00:05:07,840
Accountants call that releasing it from restriction.

93
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,090
You can just call it a promise kept.

94
00:05:10,090 --> 00:05:14,479
Because the goal is different, the reports look different too.

95
00:05:14,479 --> 00:05:16,673
A business has a balance sheet.

96
00:05:16,673 --> 00:05:20,212
A nonprofit has a statement of financial position.

97
00:05:20,212 --> 00:05:21,628
Same idea, new name.

98
00:05:21,628 --> 00:05:24,106
A business has an income statement.

99
00:05:24,106 --> 00:05:30,972
A nonprofit has a statement of activities, which just lists what came in and what it went toward.

100
00:05:30,972 --> 00:05:34,157
And a business has owner's equity, or profit.

101
00:05:34,157 --> 00:05:36,069
A nonprofit has net assets.

102
00:05:36,069 --> 00:05:39,962
There are no owners to pay, so there is no profit line.

103
00:05:39,962 --> 00:05:42,085
What's left over isn't profit.

104
00:05:42,085 --> 00:05:46,049
It's net assets, carried forward to keep doing the work.

105
00:05:46,049 --> 00:05:49,164
One quick aside, so I'm not hiding anything.

106
00:05:49,164 --> 00:05:52,633
Governments use a version of fund accounting too.

107
00:05:52,633 --> 00:05:57,163
It runs under a different rulebook, with its own kinds of funds.

108
00:05:57,163 --> 00:05:58,862
That's a separate world.

109
00:05:58,862 --> 00:06:01,060
Everything here is about nonprofits.

110
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:02,878
So that's fund accounting.

111
00:06:02,878 --> 00:06:05,255
One idea sits under all the terms.

112
00:06:05,255 --> 00:06:10,778
Track money by the promise attached to it, so you can prove you kept your word.

113
00:06:10,778 --> 00:06:13,225
Promised money in its own envelope.

114
00:06:13,225 --> 00:06:16,161
Flexible money where the mission needs it.

115
00:06:16,161 --> 00:06:17,699
Two groups, not three.

116
00:06:17,699 --> 00:06:21,124
You don't need an army of accountants to do this.

117
00:06:21,124 --> 00:06:27,906
You need a system that keeps the funds separate for you, so the proof is ready when someone asks.

118
00:06:27,906 --> 00:06:31,681
That's the kind of thing we built GrantPipe to handle.

119
00:06:31,681 --> 00:06:34,477
But the idea matters more than any tool.

120
00:06:34,477 --> 00:06:39,931
If you want a head start, we made a free Restricted Fund Tracking Spreadsheet.

121
00:06:39,931 --> 00:06:42,238
It sets up the envelopes for you.

122
00:06:42,238 --> 00:06:44,195
We'll send it to your inbox.

123
00:06:44,195 --> 00:06:48,180
Thanks for watching, and good luck keeping your promises.
